WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.447
A photo, taken on a dark December night in Oxford.

00:00:03.447 --> 00:00:07.574
Can a map built on an overcast day say exactly where the camera stood?

00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:11.344
Hi, I'm Halvard!

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:15.060
Today: how a visual positioning system finds a camera from a single

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.847
picture, and how to read a demo of one.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:20.221
Chapter one.

00:00:20.221 --> 00:00:21.562
A map that remembers.

00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:27.505
Start with one overcast November drive.

00:00:27.505 --> 00:00:31.242
In every image, find key points, and describe the patch around

00:00:31.242 --> 00:00:32.600
each as a vector.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.547
Match those features between overlapping images, then triangulate each match into

00:00:37.547 --> 00:00:39.193
a point in space.

00:00:39.193 --> 00:00:42.896
Bundle adjustment refines every camera and every point together.

00:00:43.316 --> 00:00:46.470
Out comes a cloud of 3D points, each keeping the descriptors

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:47.617
it was seen with.

00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:50.198
That memory lets a new photo find it again.

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:55.826
This is the benchmark's map of an Oxford square, built from the reference drive.

00:00:56.246 --> 00:00:59.981
And these are query photos of the same building: sun, night, snow,

00:00:59.981 --> 00:01:02.160
and low sun that washes everything out.

00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:04.752
Chapter two.

00:01:04.752 --> 00:01:06.239
One photo, one pose.

00:01:09.925 --> 00:01:10.594
First, retrieval.

00:01:10.594 --> 00:01:14.610
One vector for the whole photo, compared with every map image, returns

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.949
the few most alike.

00:01:15.949 --> 00:01:17.956
A city shrinks to a street.

00:01:18.376 --> 00:01:19.397
Then local matching.

00:01:19.397 --> 00:01:23.482
Each query feature that matches a feature of a mapped point becomes

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:25.865
a correspondence: this pixel shows that point.

00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:30.972
Finally, Perspective-n-Point solves for the rotation and position that project those

00:01:30.972 --> 00:01:32.677
points onto their pixels.

00:01:32.677 --> 00:01:34.808
Three good correspondences are enough.

00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:40.537
Seen from above, the map is two rows of facade points along the street.

00:01:40.957 --> 00:01:43.563
A query photo's matches are rays from the camera.

00:01:43.563 --> 00:01:45.589
Most end on the point they claim.

00:01:45.589 --> 00:01:47.616
Some are wrong, and point at nothing.

00:01:48.036 --> 00:01:51.929
RANSAC draws three rays at random, solves a pose, and counts how

00:01:51.929 --> 00:01:53.875
many other rays that pose explains.

00:01:53.875 --> 00:01:56.146
The pose with the most support wins.

00:01:56.566 --> 00:01:58.197
At night most rays are wrong.

00:01:58.197 --> 00:02:01.459
A triple with a bad ray can win the vote, and the

00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:02.818
pose lands somewhere else entirely.

00:02:03.238 --> 00:02:05.286
Learned features bring enough good rays back.

00:02:05.286 --> 00:02:08.796
The vote finds the true pose again, and the inliers light up.

00:02:10.317 --> 00:02:13.744
This is why conditions matter: they set the share of good matches.

00:02:14.164 --> 00:02:17.948
If a share w of the matches is right, a random triple

00:02:17.948 --> 00:02:19.840
is clean with probability w cubed.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.572
N is how many triples you must draw to see one clean triple.

00:02:24.572 --> 00:02:26.563
At seventy percent good, about eleven.

00:02:26.563 --> 00:02:28.553
At ten percent, over four thousand.

00:02:30.258 --> 00:02:30.784
Chapter three.

00:02:30.784 --> 00:02:32.364
What night does to a feature.

00:02:35.383 --> 00:02:39.952
In the benchmark paper, a standard hand-crafted method placed about half of

00:02:39.952 --> 00:02:41.856
rain queries within twenty-five centimetres.

00:02:41.856 --> 00:02:43.760
At night, half a percent.

00:02:45.258 --> 00:02:47.742
With learned features, night queries can succeed.

00:02:47.742 --> 00:02:52.355
Here, a dark query and its daytime map image share many consistent matches.

00:02:52.775 --> 00:02:54.682
But there are two ways to fail.

00:02:54.682 --> 00:02:56.317
Retrieval can return the wrong place,

00:02:56.737 --> 00:02:59.720
or the right place can yield too few good matches for the vote.

00:03:01.092 --> 00:03:05.323
Today the public board's night column runs from about forty percent for

00:03:05.323 --> 00:03:08.849
the standard single-camera pipeline to sixty-six for the best row.

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:11.279
Chapter four.

00:03:11.279 --> 00:03:12.903
What MultiSet's video shows.

00:03:16.342 --> 00:03:20.207
MultiSet built its map from the overcast day, then localised photos from

00:03:20.207 --> 00:03:22.139
nine other drives across a year.

00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:25.360
Here is the dry night drive of December the tenth.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.701
Each card prints the error from the reference pose.

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:31.947
These three night queries land eighteen to twenty-four centimetres off.

00:03:33.258 --> 00:03:36.751
All twenty-seven showcased queries fall inside the finest threshold.

00:03:36.751 --> 00:03:39.467
Those are good results for those photos.

00:03:40.758 --> 00:03:43.059
But they were chosen for a video.

00:03:43.059 --> 00:03:47.004
At the best row's rates, twenty-seven random queries would all pass about

00:03:47.004 --> 00:03:48.648
once in twenty-seven thousand tries.

00:03:49.942 --> 00:03:51.383
So here is what is missing.

00:03:52.808 --> 00:03:55.678
There is no recall, the share of all queries that pass.

00:03:56.098 --> 00:03:58.768
No row on the public board connects to MultiSet.

00:03:59.188 --> 00:04:02.700
The cards need reference poses, so these are probably from the half

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:03.870
whose poses are public.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.523
And the reference poses carry their own error, about the size of

00:04:07.523 --> 00:04:08.870
the numbers on the cards.

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:11.084
Chapter five.

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:13.242
Photos that land where they were taken.

00:04:16.883 --> 00:04:20.324
At a trade show, plant and site people asked for two jobs:

00:04:20.324 --> 00:04:23.766
photos that land where they were taken, and finding the equipment they

00:04:23.766 --> 00:04:24.913
were sent to fix.

00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:29.243
On a site, the hard cases are repeated floors and a scene

00:04:29.243 --> 00:04:30.547
that changes every week.

00:04:30.547 --> 00:04:33.805
RobotCar tests neither, so measure on your own held-out photos.

00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:38.651
A VPS is retrieval, matching and a RANSAC vote.

00:04:38.651 --> 00:04:42.583
A benchmark's value is its denominator; the video left it out.

00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:48.160
The map remembers, RANSAC votes, and a demo shows successes, not a rate.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.104
Thanks for watching!

00:04:49.524 --> 00:04:51.607
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:51.607 --> 00:04:52.202
I'm Halvard.

00:04:52.202 --> 00:04:52.500
Bye!
